# The abstraction: two resources, two holders, four job states each, epochs
# 1..=4 and generations 1..=2. Every step names one resource, so the search
# covers interleavings across resources. `take_over(e)` is accepted only when
# `e` strictly exceeds that resource's current epoch; a generation advance keeps
# the epoch and a takeover starts generation 1 at the new one.
#
# The four claims checked on every reachable state and every transition:
#   E1 a takeover is accepted only under a strictly greater epoch;
#   E2 no accepted transition lowers the committed epoch;
#   E3 a committed fence never has epoch 0;
#   E4 a takeover installs generation 1 at the new epoch.
#
# Differential conformance drives the real JobQueue with the same steps on both
# resources and compares the accept/refuse answer and the observable state
# (both fences, holders, released flags, job states) after every step.

$ cargo test --offline -p unfer_consensus --features network --test epoch_invariant -- --nocapture
